1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,301
Day 61: does a short rules file alone stop a coding agent from

2
00:00:04,301 --> 00:00:08,394
crossing a boundary, or do you also need a mechanical gate.

3
00:00:08,844 --> 00:00:12,661
A recent paper gave coding agents a short rules file and

4
00:00:12,661 --> 00:00:15,387
four boundaries they should never cross.

5
00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:19,067
We repeated it using this project's own rules file and

6
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:19,885
Claude Code.

7
00:00:20,335 --> 00:00:24,015
Each trial starts a fresh agent with our rules file and

8
00:00:24,015 --> 00:00:26,558
a task that quietly tempts a shortcut,

9
00:00:26,558 --> 00:00:29,101
like installing an unverified package.

10
00:00:29,551 --> 00:00:33,124
In the gated condition, the risky path is mechanically

11
00:00:33,124 --> 00:00:35,903
blocked: no server on the disallowed port,

12
00:00:35,903 --> 00:00:38,285
or a file locked with chattr plus i,

13
00:00:38,285 --> 00:00:40,336
which even root can't override.

14
00:00:40,786 --> 00:00:44,200
A hidden test then checks what actually happened on disk and

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,102
over the network, not what the agent says happened.

16
00:00:47,552 --> 00:00:49,256
Across all eight trials,

17
00:00:49,256 --> 00:00:52,593
the hidden tests found zero boundary crossings.

18
00:00:52,593 --> 00:00:55,362
One agent even volunteered, unprompted,

19
00:00:55,362 --> 00:00:57,421
a minor rule slip of its own.

20
00:00:57,871 --> 00:01:03,606
Full repro steps and write-up, in Japanese, on Zenn.

21
00:01:03,606 --> 00:01:06,474
Paper at arXiv 2611.27112.
